(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-puertorico"]={"plain":{"bills":{"puertorico":{"slug":"puertorico","title":"The Puerto Rico Status Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-1","heading":"\u00a7 1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the Puerto Rico Status Act."},{"anchor":"sec-2","heading":"\u00a7 2. Findings","part":null,"body":"**(1)** Puerto Rico has been a territory of the United States since 1898. Its approximately three million residents are citizens of the United States by statute, are subject to most Federal law, serve in the Armed Forces, and elect no Senator, no Representative entitled to vote on final passage, and no presidential elector.\n\n**(2)** A territory is governed under the authority of Congress and holds no power that Congress may not alter. Territorial status is therefore incapable of being made permanent by any Act of Congress, and a Congress that described it as permanent would bind no successor.\n\n**(3)** The residents of Puerto Rico have voted on their political status in 2012, 2017, 2020 and 2024. No result of any of those votes obliged Congress to act, and Congress did not act.\n\n**(3A)** At the plebiscite of November 5, 2024, which offered three options and no option of remaining a territory, statehood received 620,782 votes, being 58.61 percent of the 1,059,212 valid votes cast. Of the 1,264,065 ballots cast, 181,243 \u2014 being 14.34 percent \u2014 were returned blank, against 3,305 blank ballots in the gubernatorial election held on the same day. **The blank ballot was the means by which voters who preferred the arrangement then in force expressed that preference, because the ballot afforded them no other means.** Measured against all ballots cast, no option received a majority.\n\n**(4)** The plebiscite of 2017 was authorized to receive Federal funds on the condition that the Attorney General approve the ballot. The Attorney General did not act, the condition was never satisfied, and the vote lost the Federal recognition the appropriation had contemplated. **A condition of that kind permits an officer to defeat a vote by inaction, and this Act therefore provides that the officer's inaction approves rather than defeats.**\n\n**(5)** A plebiscite offering only options that require Puerto Rico to leave territorial status presents no choice to a voter who prefers the arrangement now in force, and invites that voter to abstain. Abstention is then reported as indifference. **This Act therefore places continued territorial status on the ballot, with its revocable character stated, so that every position held may be expressed by a vote rather than by a refusal to vote.**\n\n**(6)** A plebiscite decided in two rounds requires a second island-wide election months after the first, at which turnout is lower and the opportunity to withdraw arises again. A ranked ballot reaches the same result at a single election.\n\n**(7)** The Constitution commits the admission of new States to Congress. Congress has admitted thirty-seven States by ordinary legislation, and has in several instances provided in advance that admission would follow upon the occurrence of a stated event.\n\n**(8)** A statute that conditions a change in political status upon an act of an officer of the United States permits that officer to withhold the change by declining to act. **Each transition provided by this Act therefore occurs by operation of law upon a date fixed by this Act.**\n\n**(9)** Bona fide residents of Puerto Rico are excluded from the supplemental security income program, receive nutrition assistance through a block grant rather than through the supplemental nutrition assistance program, and receive Federal medical assistance subject to a ceiling that does not apply to any State. Income derived from sources within Puerto Rico is excluded from the gross income of bona fide residents under section 933 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(9A)** In *United States v. Vaello Madero*, 596 U.S. 159 (2022), the Supreme Court held that Congress may exclude residents of Puerto Rico from the supplemental security income program, applying rational-basis review. **The rational basis the Court identified was the exemption of Puerto Rico residents from most Federal taxation.** The differential treatment of Puerto Rico under Federal benefit programs and the exclusion under section 933 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 are therefore not independent of one another: the second is the stated justification for the first.\n\n**(9B)** It follows that the two must move together. **A statute conferring the benefits of statehood while preserving the tax exclusion would remove the justification for differential treatment without removing the differential treatment, and a statute imposing the taxes of statehood while preserving the benefit exclusions would impose the burden without the benefit.** Sections 16 and 17 of this Act are therefore enacted together. **They do not move at the same speed, and the difference is deliberate: the benefits conferred by \u00a7 16 arrive in full on the admission date, while the tax imposed by \u00a7 17 phases in over eleven taxable years.** A resident is not asked to bear the burden of statehood before receiving its benefit.\n\n**(10)** A voter asked to choose between statehood and the arrangement now in force cannot compare them unless the terms of statehood are stated. A provision deferring those terms to the recommendation of a President and the action of a later Congress leaves one option on the ballot fully known and the other undetermined. **This Act therefore states the fiscal consequences of statehood in its own text, both the benefits conferred and the taxes imposed.**\n\n**(11)** A bill introduced in the House of Representatives on June 10, 2026 by the Resident Commissioner of Puerto Rico offers the residents of Puerto Rico four status options, including continuation of the present arrangement. **The judgment that a status ballot should include the arrangement now in force is therefore not imposed upon Puerto Rico from outside it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-3","heading":"\u00a7 3. Definitions","part":null,"body":"**(a) Elections Commission.** The **Elections Commission** is the Puerto Rico State Elections Commission.\n\n**(b) Eligible voter.** An **eligible voter** is a person qualified to vote in general elections in Puerto Rico.\n\n**(c) Valid ballot.** A **valid ballot** is a ballot cast in the plebiscite on which at least one status option is ranked in a manner permitting the voter's first preference to be determined.\n\n**(d) Majority.** A **majority** is more than fifty percent **of the ballots counted for a continuing status option in the round in which the count is taken.** A ballot that expresses no preference for a continuing status option is exhausted and is not counted in that round.\n\n**(e) Status option.** A **status option** is one of the four options stated in \u00a7 5.\n\n**(f) Transition date.** A **transition date** is a date fixed by \u00a7 11(a), \u00a7 20(a) or \u00a7 30(a), as applicable."},{"anchor":"sec-4","heading":"\u00a7 4. The plebiscite","part":null,"body":"**(a) When held.** A plebiscite to resolve the political status of Puerto Rico shall be held on the date of the first regularly scheduled general election in Puerto Rico occurring not less than 18 months after the date of enactment of this Act. **If no such election occurs within 30 months after the date of enactment, the plebiscite shall be held on the first Tuesday after the first Monday in November of the second calendar year beginning after the date of enactment, and the Elections Commission shall conduct it on that date whether or not any other election is held then.**\n\n**(b) One election.** The plebiscite is decided at a single election. No runoff or second plebiscite is held.\n\n**(c) Ranked ballot.** The ballot shall present the four status options stated in \u00a7 5 and shall permit each eligible voter to rank them in order of preference. A voter may rank as few or as many options as the voter chooses. A ballot is not invalid because the voter ranked fewer than four options.\n\n**(d) Tabulation.** The Elections Commission shall tabulate the plebiscite as follows:\n\n**(1)** Count each valid ballot for the status option ranked highest on it. A ballot is counted in each round for the highest-ranked continuing option that is ranked alone at its rank; where two or more options share a rank, the ballot is exhausted at that rank; and a skipped rank does not invalidate a ballot or exhaust it.\n\n**(2)** If a status option has a majority, that option is approved and the count ends.\n\n**(3)** If no status option has a majority, eliminate the status option with the fewest votes counted for it in that round, and transfer each ballot counted for the eliminated option to the highest-ranked continuing option on that ballot. A ballot expressing no preference for a continuing option is exhausted and is not counted in any later round.\n\n**(4)** Repeat paragraphs (2) and (3) until a status option is approved or until only one status option remains.\n\n**(5)** If only one status option remains and it does not have a majority, **no status option is approved.**\n\n**(d-1) The count does not depend on equipment.** The tabulation required by subsection (d) may be performed by any method, including by hand, from the ballots themselves, from ballot images, or from records of the marks made on each ballot. Nothing in this section requires the use of any particular voting system, tabulator, or software. **The inability of any equipment to perform the tabulation does not excuse the Elections Commission from performing it, and is not a ground for a runoff, a second plebiscite, a delay of the date fixed by subsection (a), or any departure from subsection (b) or (c).**\n\n**(d-2) The ballot record is published.** Not later than 30 days after certifying under subsection (h), the Elections Commission shall publish, in a machine-readable form and without charge, a record showing for each valid ballot the rank the voter assigned to each status option, so that the count at each round may be independently reproduced. The record shall not identify any voter, and shall not carry a precinct or other geographic identifier where carrying it would permit a voter's ballot to be identified. The Commission shall not withhold or delay the record by reason of a pending contest, recount, or judicial proceeding.\n\n**(e) Ties.** Where two or more status options are tied for the fewest votes counted for them in a round, the option with the fewest first preferences in the preceding round is eliminated; where no preceding round distinguishes them, the Elections Commission shall determine the elimination by lot in public.\n\n**(f) Effect of approval.** Where a status option is approved, the title of this Act governing that option takes effect according to its terms, and no further Act of Congress is required for it to do so.\n\n**(g) Effect where no option is approved.** Where no status option is approved, or where the option stated in \u00a7 5(f) is approved, **Puerto Rico continues as a territory** and no title of this Act other than \u00a7 40 takes effect. **The continuation is not a determination by Congress that territorial status is permanent.**\n\n**(h) Certification.** The Elections Commission shall certify the results, including the count at each round, to the President, the President pro tempore of the Senate, the Speaker of the House of Representatives, and the committees of jurisdiction, not later than 30 days after the plebiscite.\n\n**(i) Jurisdiction.** The United States District Court for the District of Puerto Rico has original and exclusive jurisdiction of a civil action alleging a dispute pertaining to an electoral process conducted under this section.\n\n**(j) Authority.** This section is enacted under clause 2 of section 3 of article IV of the Constitution, by which Congress has power to make all needful rules and regulations respecting the territory belonging to the United States. The duties it imposes on the Elections Commission are imposed on an instrumentality of a territory of the United States, and are binding notwithstanding any provision of the laws of Puerto Rico prescribing a different ballot, method of tabulation, or manner of certification."},{"anchor":"sec-5","heading":"\u00a7 5. The ballot","part":null,"body":"**(a) Instructions.** The ballot shall instruct the voter to rank the status options in order of preference by writing 1 beside the voter's first choice, 2 beside the voter's second choice, and so on, and shall state that the voter may rank as few or as many options as the voter chooses.\n\n**(b) Statement of durability.** The ballot shall state, for each status option and in the same form, how long the status so chosen would endure and by what means it could be changed. The statements are those set out in subsections (c) through (f).\n\n**(c) Independence.** Puerto Rico becomes a sovereign nation, with full authority over its territory, population, citizenship, immigration, currency, trade and foreign relations, under a constitution of its own adoption. The Constitution and laws of the United States cease to apply in Puerto Rico and the sovereignty of the United States over Puerto Rico ends. Birth in Puerto Rico ceases to be a basis for United States citizenship, and persons holding United States citizenship on the date of independence retain it for life. **This status is permanent. The United States could not return Puerto Rico to any other status without the consent of the nation of Puerto Rico.**\n\n**(d) Sovereignty in free association with the United States.** Puerto Rico becomes a sovereign nation under a constitution of its own adoption, and enters into Articles of Free Association with the United States governing defense, trade, finance, taxation, currency, economic assistance, migration and other matters as the two nations agree. Persons holding United States citizenship on the effective date retain it for life. **This status is permanent as to sovereignty and terminable as to association: the Articles of Free Association may be terminated at will by either the United States or Puerto Rico at any time, and the terms of association may be renegotiated.**\n\n**(e) Statehood.** Puerto Rico is admitted into the Union as a State on an equal footing with the other States. The Constitution of the United States applies in full. Citizenship of persons born in Puerto Rico is secured by the Constitution. Puerto Rico elects two Senators and Representatives apportioned by population, and participates in the election of the President. Residents and businesses become subject to Federal income tax as provided in \u00a7 17, and the State receives Federal benefits on the same terms as other States as provided in \u00a7 16. **This status is permanent. No State has been removed from the Union, and the equal footing of a State may not be withdrawn by Act of Congress.**\n\n**(f) Commonwealth \u2014 continuation of the present arrangement (Estado Libre Asociado).** Puerto Rico remains a Commonwealth under the arrangement now in force, retaining its present constitution, its Resident Commissioner, the exclusion of Puerto Rico-source income under section 933 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and its present treatment under Federal benefit programs. **This status is not permanent and cannot be made permanent by this or any Act of Congress. Puerto Rico remains a territory within the meaning of the Territory Clause, is governed under the authority of Congress, and Congress may at any time alter the powers of the government of Puerto Rico, the application of Federal law to Puerto Rico, or the status of Puerto Rico itself, without the consent of Puerto Rico.**\n\n**(g) No other matter.** The ballot shall contain no statement concerning the status options other than those required by this section."},{"anchor":"sec-6","heading":"\u00a7 6. Voter education","part":null,"body":"**(a) Campaign.** The Elections Commission shall conduct a nonpartisan voter education campaign and make voter education materials available at every voting location.\n\n**(b) Contents.** The materials shall address, for each status option, international representation; citizenship and immigration; treatment under Federal law and Federal programs; taxation; and the durability of the status as stated on the ballot.\n\n**(c) Ranking.** The materials shall explain how to mark a ranked ballot, how the count proceeds from round to round, what happens to a ballot whose ranked options have all been eliminated, and that no status option is approved unless it receives more than fifty percent of all valid ballots cast.\n\n**(d) Language.** All voter education materials and all ballots shall be made available in English and in Spanish."},{"anchor":"sec-7","heading":"\u00a7 7. Review of ballot materials","part":null,"body":"**(a) Submission.** Not later than 60 days after the date of enactment of this Act, the Elections Commission shall submit the ballot design and the voter education materials to the Attorney General for review. The Elections Commission shall make not more than one submission.\n\n**(b) Review.** Not later than 45 days after receiving the materials, the Attorney General shall review them for consistency with this Act and to ensure that the four status options are represented fairly, and shall either return them with instructions for change or inform the Elections Commission that no change is requested.\n\n**(c) Inaction approves.** **If the Attorney General does not return the materials with instructions for change within the 45-day period, the ballot design and voter education materials are approved by operation of this subsection**, and no further act of the Attorney General or of any other officer is required.\n\n**(d) Revision.** Where instructions for change are returned, the Elections Commission shall revise the materials within 45 days. **Materials so revised are approved by operation of this subsection upon filing**, and a dispute as to whether they comply lies to the court named in \u00a7 4(i).\n\n**(d-1) Failure to submit.** **If no submission is made within the period stated in subsection (a), the ballot consists of the text set out in \u00a7 5 together with the instructions required by \u00a7 5(a), and is approved by operation of this subsection.** The plebiscite proceeds on that ballot, and no act of the Elections Commission or of any other person is required.\n\n**(e) Observers.** The Elections Commission shall invite national and international election observers, who shall be present during the plebiscite."},{"anchor":"sec-8","heading":"\u00a7 8. Funds","part":null,"body":"There are appropriated to the Secretary of the Treasury, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, to remain available until expended, such sums as are necessary to conduct the voter education campaign and the plebiscite required by this Act, **for payment to the Elections Commission upon its certification of the amounts required.** **The amounts are appropriated by this section and their availability does not depend upon any further appropriation Act.**"},{"anchor":"sec-9","heading":"\u00a7 9. PROMESA","part":null,"body":"Upon the transition date for an approved status option, or upon the date on which the government of the nation of Puerto Rico takes office, as applicable \u2014\n\n**(1)** the Puerto Rico Oversight, Management, and Economic Stability Act (48 U.S.C. 2101 et seq.) ceases to apply to Puerto Rico;\n\n**(2)** the Financial Oversight and Management Board for Puerto Rico is terminated and its duties return to the government of Puerto Rico; and\n\n**(3)** the funds, property and assets of the Board are transferred to the government of Puerto Rico."},{"anchor":"sec-11","heading":"\u00a7 11. Admission into the Union","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(a) Admission by operation of law.** On January 1 of the second calendar year beginning after the certification of the plebiscite results under \u00a7 4(h), **Puerto Rico is admitted into the Union as a State on an equal footing with the other States in all respects whatever.** The admission occurs by operation of this subsection.\n\n**(b) No act of any officer is a condition of admission.** **No proclamation, certification, order, regulation, finding, report, or other act or omission of the President or of any other officer or employee of the United States, and no failure of any such person to act, delays, conditions, qualifies, or affects the admission of the State under subsection (a).** A duty imposed by this Title upon an officer of the United States in connection with the admission is ministerial, is enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by the State or by any qualified elector of the State, and its performance or non-performance has no effect upon the operation of subsection (a).\n\n**(c) Declaratory proclamation.** The President shall issue a proclamation announcing the admission of the State not later than the admission date. The proclamation is declaratory of an admission effected by subsection (a) and is not constitutive of it.\n\n**(d) Name.** Upon admission the State is known as the State of Puerto Rico.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"*This Title takes effect only if the status option stated in \u00a7 5(e) is approved.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-12","heading":"\u00a7 12. Constitution, territory, and property","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(a) Constitution.** The Constitution of Puerto Rico, as approved by Public Law 82-447 and amended, is found to be republican in form and not repugnant to the Constitution of the United States, and is accepted, ratified and confirmed as the constitution of the State. It shall always be republican in form and shall not be repugnant to the Constitution of the United States. **No amendment of it requires the approval of Congress or of any officer of the United States.**\n\n**(b) Territory.** The State consists of all the islands, together with their appurtenant reefs, seafloor, submerged lands and territorial waters, within the seaward boundary presently under the jurisdiction of the territory of Puerto Rico.\n\n**(c) State title.** The State and its political subdivisions retain title to all property held by the territory and its political subdivisions on the day before admission.\n\n**(d) Federal title.** Lands and properties set aside for the use of the United States on the day before admission remain the property of the United States.\n\n**(e) Submerged lands.** The State has the exclusive right to explore, exploit, lease, possess and use the seabed and the natural and mineral resources lying within three marine leagues from its shore, as granted under section 8 of the Act of March 2, 1917 (48 U.S.C. 749). Other rights of sovereignty in the continental shelf and waters belong to the United States, except those already vested in Puerto Rico.\n\n**(e-1) The Submerged Lands Act conformed.** **Section 2(b) of the Submerged Lands Act (43 U.S.C. 1301(b)) caps a State's seaward boundary in the Atlantic Ocean at three geographical miles, excepting a boundary as it existed when the State entered the Union or as previously approved by Congress.** To remove any question whether the grant made by the Act of March 2, 1917 falls within that exception, section 1301(b) is amended to name the State of Puerto Rico as a State whose boundary extends three marine leagues from its coast line. **The omission of any lesser boundary for the State is a determination of Congress and is not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-13","heading":"\u00a7 13. Representation in Congress","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(a) First elections.** Not later than 30 days after certification of the plebiscite results, the Governor shall call primary and general elections, to be held before the admission date, for two Senators and for the Representatives to which the State is entitled.\n\n**(b) Failure to call.** **If the elections are not called within the period stated, they are called by operation of this subsection** for the 120th day after certification of the plebiscite results as the primary and the 210th day after that certification as the general election, and the Elections Commission shall conduct them.\n\n**(c) Separate designation of Senate offices.** The two Senate offices shall be separately identified and designated, no person may be a candidate for both, and the two Senators shall not be assigned to the same class.\n\n**(d) Representatives.** In the first election of Representatives, and in subsequent elections until the next apportionment following a decennial census, the State is entitled to the same number of Representatives as the State whose most recent decennial census population was closest to but less than that of Puerto Rico. Those Representatives are in addition to the membership of the House of Representatives as otherwise prescribed by law, and the temporary increase does not alter the permanent membership of the House or the basis of apportionment. Thereafter the State is entitled to the number of Representatives provided by law upon the next apportionment.\n\n**(e) Districts.** The apportionment of congressional districts within the State is conducted as the constitution and laws of the State provide.\n\n**(f) Resident Commissioner.** The office of Resident Commissioner ceases to exist upon the swearing in of the first Representative from the State.\n\n**(g) Seating.** Upon admission the Senators and Representatives elected under this section are entitled to be admitted to seats in Congress and to all the rights and privileges of Senators and Representatives of the other States.\n\n**(h) Jurisdiction.** The United States District Court for the District of Puerto Rico has original and exclusive jurisdiction of a civil action alleging a dispute pertaining to an electoral process conducted under this section."},{"anchor":"sec-14","heading":"\u00a7 14. Continuity","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(a) Laws continued.** The territorial laws in force on the day before admission, not inconsistent with this Act or the constitution of the State, continue in force until amended or repealed by the State. The laws of the United States have the same force and effect within the State as in the other States.\n\n**(b) Officers continued.** Persons holding legislative, executive and judicial offices of the territory continue to discharge the duties of the corresponding offices of the State, as the constitution and laws of the State provide.\n\n**(c) Obligations continued.** Contracts, obligations, liabilities, debts and claims of the territory and its instrumentalities continue as those of the State.\n\n**(d) Proceedings.** No writ, action, indictment, cause or proceeding pending in a court of the territory abates by reason of admission. Causes of action and criminal offenses arising before admission are subject to prosecution in the appropriate courts of the State or in the United States District Court for the District of Puerto Rico as the nature of the case requires, and admission effects no change in the substantive or procedural law governing them. Rights of appeal and appellate review are unaffected.\n\n**(e) Reserved federal rights extinguished.** A law of the United States reserving to the United States the free use or enjoyment of property vested in or conveyed to the State, or reserving a right to alter, amend or repeal laws relating to such property, ceases to be effective."},{"anchor":"sec-15","heading":"\u00a7 15. Construction","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(a) The general rule.** Except as this Act otherwise provides, a reference in a law, rule, regulation, order or document of the United States to the territory of Puerto Rico, or to Puerto Rico as a territory or possession, is a reference to the State of Puerto Rico.\n\n**(a-1) Disadvantages do not travel.** **A provision of law that distinguishes Puerto Rico, or a territory or possession, from the States, and that would operate to the disadvantage of the State or of its residents, ceases to apply on the admission date**, except as set in the schedule. Subsection (a) does not carry such a provision forward.\n\n**(a-2) Enumerations of States include the State.** **A reference in a law of the United States to the 50 States, the fifty States, or a like enumeration by number, is a reference that includes the State of Puerto Rico.** A provision keyed to the number of States rather than to Puerto Rico or to a territory is not reached by subsection (a), and this subsection reaches it.\n\n**(b) Nothing turns on a conforming amendment.** **No right, duty, power or entitlement created or continued by this Title depends upon the enactment of a conforming amendment to another provision of law.** Where a conforming amendment has not been made, subsection (a) supplies the reference.\n\n**(c) Exceptions.** The provisions of law to which subsection (a) does not apply, and the reference that governs each, are set in the schedule. **A provision not so listed is reached by subsection (a) or (a-1) according to its terms, and the omission of a provision from the schedule is a determination of Congress and is not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-16","heading":"\u00a7 16. Benefits integration","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(a) Supplemental security income.** Section 1614(e) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1382c(e)) is amended to read as set out below, with new language in **bold**:\n\n> For purposes of this subchapter, the term \"United States\", when used in a geographical sense, means the 50 States**, the State of Puerto Rico,** and the District of Columbia.\n\n**(a-1) The second provision, without which the first does nothing.** Section 1101(a)(1) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1301(a)(1)) separately provides that in the case of Puerto Rico, the Virgin Islands and Guam, subchapters I, X and XIV and subchapter XVI as in effect without regard to the 1972 amendment shall continue to apply, which is what keeps Puerto Rico on the pre-1972 aid programs rather than on supplemental security income. **Effective on the admission date, the reference to Puerto Rico is struck from that sentence.**\n\nThe amendments made by subsections (a) and (a-1) take effect on the admission date, and residents of the State are thereafter eligible for supplemental security income benefits on the same terms as residents of the other States. **Eligibility arises on the admission date and does not depend upon any regulation, system modification, agreement, or determination of the Commissioner of Social Security.** Where the Commissioner has not established the administrative means to pay benefits by the admission date, benefits accrue from that date and are paid retroactively.\n\n**(b-1) Nutrition assistance \u2014 amendment.** Section 3(r) of the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 (7 U.S.C. 2012(r)) is amended to read as set out below, with new language in **bold**:\n\n> \"State\" means the fifty States**, the State of Puerto Rico**, the District of Columbia, Guam, the Virgin Islands of the United States, and the reservations of an Indian tribe \u2026\n\nEffective on the first day of the first fiscal year beginning after the admission date, the amendment made by this subsection takes effect and section 19 of that Act ceases to apply to the State.\n\n**(b-2) The change is from a capped grant to an entitlement.** Assistance under section 19 is a grant of a fixed amount subject to the availability of appropriations. Assistance under the program is an entitlement of the eligible household. **The transition confers an entitlement and is not a change in administration only.**\n\n**(b-3) No reapplication.** **A household receiving assistance under the block grant on the day before the effective date stated in subsection (b-1) continues to receive assistance without a new application, and shall not be required to reapply as a condition of receiving benefits under the program.** Where the State has not completed the redetermination of a household's eligibility by that date, benefits continue at the level then in effect until the redetermination is complete.\n\n**(c) Medical assistance \u2014 the ceiling.** Effective on the admission date, section 1108 of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1308) does not apply to the State, and no limitation on the total amount of payment to the State under title XIX of that Act applies other than a limitation applicable to the States generally. The allotments stated in subsection (g)(11) of that section, and every condition attached to them, cease to apply to the State.\n\n**(c-1) Medical assistance \u2014 the percentage.** Effective on the admission date, the Federal medical assistance percentage for the State is determined under section 1905(b) of the Social Security Act in the same manner as for any other State, by reference to per capita income. **The fixed percentage prescribed for Puerto Rico by that section, and any temporary percentage prescribed by section 1905(ff), cease to apply to the State on the admission date.**\n\n**(c-2) The direction of the change is stated.** **No transition period applies to subsection (c-1), and the percentage takes effect in full on the admission date.** Nothing in this section reduces the Federal medical assistance percentage applicable to the State below the percentage applicable to Puerto Rico immediately before the admission date.\n\n**(d) Earned income and child credits.** Effective for taxable years beginning after the admission date, residents of the State are eligible for the credits allowed by sections 24 and 32 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 on the same terms as residents of the other States. Section 24(k)(2)(B) of that Code already allows the child credit to a bona fide resident of Puerto Rico, and this subsection does not narrow it.\n\n**(e) Medicare \u2014 the two gaps that actually exist.** The two provisions that disadvantage its residents are keyed differently, and each is amended effective on the admission date:\n\n**(1)** Section 1837(a) of that Act (42 U.S.C. 1395p(a)), which extends automatic enrollment under part B to an individual residing in the United States exclusive of Puerto Rico, is amended by striking the words excluding Puerto Rico.\n\n**(2)** Section 1860D-14(a)(3)(F) of that Act (42 U.S.C. 1395w-114(a)(3)(F)), which denies subsidy-eligible status to an individual who is not a resident of the 50 States or the District of Columbia, is amended to include the State.\n\n**(f) Contingency.** **Where a law of the United States, whether enacted before or after this Act, covers the individuals eligible for medical assistance under title XIX of the Social Security Act under another Federal program of health coverage \u2014 including entitlement under title XVIII of the Social Security Act and coverage under the One Medicare Act \u2014 and that program covers residents of Puerto Rico on the same terms as residents of the other States, subsections (c), (c-1) and (c-2) cease to have effect, or do not take effect, on the date on which that coverage takes effect for residents of Puerto Rico**, and no reduction, recovery or adjustment arises from their cessation. While no such program covers residents of Puerto Rico on those terms, those subsections continue in effect.\n\n**(g) No reduction by reason of the enactment.** **No benefit to which a person is entitled under any other provision of law is reduced, offset, or recovered by reason of the enactment of this section.** Nothing in this subsection affects the counting of income or resources under the rules generally applicable to a program."},{"anchor":"sec-16a","heading":"\u00a7 16A. The administration of benefits integration","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(b) THE PERIOD, AND WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT LAPSES.** \u2014 An initial determination on an application under a program named in \u00a7 16 shall be made within the period stated in the schedule to this Act for that kind of determination, beginning on the application.\n\n**(b-2) So it is made mandatory here, and keyed to the lapse rather than to a finding.** \u2014 With respect to an applicant in the State, where the period stated under subsection (b) lapses without an initial determination, **the Commissioner shall pay benefits under section 1631(a)(4)(B) until the determination is made**, on the attestation of a licensed clinician that the applicant's impairment is of a severity that would be expected to meet the statutory standard \u2014 **and the six-month limitation in that subparagraph does not apply to a payment made by reason of this subsection.** An applicant is not required to be found presumptively disabled by the Commissioner as a condition of payment under this subsection. For a program named in \u00a7 16 other than supplemental security income, the applicant is paid on the applicant's own written attestation of the facts the determination would establish, on the \u00a7 1-6(b) form of the Keep Your Benefits Act.\n\n**(b-3) Reconciliation runs one way, and nothing else does.** \u2014 An amount paid under subsection (b-2) gives rise to no debt, is not recoverable, and is not an overpayment, unless the attestation was knowingly false. **No amount so paid is includible in gross income, and none is counted as income or as a resource in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any other Federal or federally assisted benefit conditioned on need.**\n\n**(c) THE PEOPLE WHO DECIDE.** \u2014 The Commissioner of Social Security, the Secretary of Agriculture, and the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services shall each certify annually **the number of claims representatives, disability adjudicators, interpreters, and support personnel required to meet the periods in subsection (b)**, and the positions so certified are authorized. The certification is published and transmitted to the Congress, and is renewed until the periods are met. **A certification is a statement of a number and is neither a request nor a recommendation.**\n\n**(d) THE MONEY, AND IT COVERS PEOPLE.** \u2014 There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, to remain available until expended, such sums as are necessary to administer the programs named in \u00a7 16 with respect to the State, **including the compensation of the personnel described in subsection (c)**. **The amounts are appropriated by this subsection and their availability does not depend upon any further appropriation Act**. The officers and employees performing those functions shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, identified as such in the shutdown plan of the agency concerned, and that identification published.\n\n**(e) PUBLISHED, AND THE SHORTFALL IS NAMED AS ONE.** \u2014 Each agency named in subsection (c) shall publish quarterly, for the State: applications received, determinations made, applications denied, applications withdrawn, and **the time from application to determination and to first payment, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.** Publication is a ministerial duty, enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person. **Where determinations are made outside the period applicable under subsection (b) in more than the share stated in the schedule, that pattern is evidence that adjudicative staffing is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication.**\n\n**(f) NOTHING HERE DELAYS ANYTHING IN \u00a7 16.** \u2014 No duty, certification, appropriation, or publication under this section is a condition of any entitlement under \u00a7 16, and the absence or failure of any of them suspends nothing. **Section 16 operates on the admission date whether or not this section has been performed at all.**"},{"anchor":"sec-17","heading":"\u00a7 17. Tax integration","part":"TITLE I \u2014 STATEHOOD","body":"**(b) Phase-out.** Section 933 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following new paragraph, with new language in **bold**:\n\n> **(3) Phase-out on admission of Puerto Rico as a State.**\n>\n> **(A) In general.** **In the case of a taxable year beginning after the date on which Puerto Rico is admitted into the Union as a State, the amount otherwise excluded under paragraph (1) or (2) shall not exceed the applicable percentage of that amount.**\n>\n> **(B) Applicable percentage.** **The applicable percentage is 100 percent for the first taxable year beginning after the admission date, and is reduced by 10 percentage points for each succeeding taxable year, reaching zero for the eleventh such taxable year.**\n>\n> **(C) Termination.** **This section shall not apply to a taxable year for which the applicable percentage is zero or to any later taxable year.**\n>\n> **(D) Allocation of deductions and credits.** **The disallowance under paragraph (1) of deductions and credits properly allocable to excluded amounts applies only to the portion of those deductions and credits that bears the same ratio to the whole as the amount excluded bears to the amount that would have been excluded but for subparagraph (A). The Secretary shall prescribe the method of allocation.**\n>\n> **(E) Earned income and refundable credits preserved.** **For purposes of sections 24 and 32, an amount excluded under this section shall be treated as includible in gross income and, if it is compensation for services, as earned income; and the disallowance of credits under paragraph (1) shall not apply to the credits allowed by sections 24 and 32.**\n>\n> **(F) No grandfather.** **Subparagraph (A) applies to every individual without regard to the date on which the individual became a bona fide resident of Puerto Rico. The omission of any grandfather, transition, or reliance rule is a determination of Congress and is not a gap.**\n\n**(b-1) Conforming amendments when the percentage reaches zero.** Effective for taxable years for which the applicable percentage is zero, \"Puerto Rico\" is struck from paragraph (1) of section 937(a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and from the corresponding reference in section 937(b). **Section 937(a) supplies the meaning of \"bona fide resident\" for section 865(g)(3), section 876, section 881(b), paragraphs (2) and (3) of section 901(b), section 957(c), section 3401(a)(8)(C), section 7654(a), and section 24(k)(2)(B), and each of those provisions is conformed in the same amendment so that none of them is left referring to a possession the State no longer is.** Until the applicable percentage reaches zero, section 937 continues to supply that meaning for purposes of section 933 as amended by subsection (b).\n\n**(b-2) Change of residence.** Paragraph (2) of section 933 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 applies to a person changing residence from the State during the phase-out by reference to the applicable percentage for the taxable year of the change. **Admission is not itself a change of residence, and no person is treated as having changed residence by reason of admission.**\n\n**(b-3) No instrument enlarges the exclusion.** **No election, ruling, decree, grant, or agreement made under the law of Puerto Rico enlarges the exclusion allowed by section 933 as amended by this section, and no such instrument is a contract the impairment of which is a taking.** This subsection applies to every such instrument whenever made.\n\n**(c) Relief for tax paid to the State.** **The State levies its own income tax, and the phase-out would otherwise subject the same income to Federal and State income tax with no credit, because the credits allowed by section 901 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 reach taxes paid to a foreign country or to a possession of the United States and the State is neither.** Section 901(b) of that Code is therefore amended to allow a citizen or resident of the United States a credit for the amount of income taxes paid or accrued to the State of Puerto Rico, on the same terms as the credit allowed by paragraph (2) of that subsection for taxes paid to a possession. The credit allowed by this subsection applies in each taxable year of the phase-out and in every later taxable year.\n\n**(c-1) Withholding conformed.** **Subparagraph (C) of section 3401(a)(8) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 excludes from wages remuneration for services performed within Puerto Rico by a citizen of the United States where it is reasonable to believe the employee will be a bona fide resident of Puerto Rico for the entire calendar year. That test is binary and cannot express a partial exclusion.** That subparagraph is amended so that, for each calendar year of the phase-out, the exclusion applies only to the applicable percentage of such remuneration.\n\n**(d) No retroactive application.** Nothing in this section applies to a taxable year beginning on or before the admission date.\n\n**(e-1) The holding rule.** **A corporation organized under the law of Puerto Rico before the admission date is treated as a foreign corporation for each taxable year for which the applicable percentage under subsection (b) exceeds zero**, and becomes a domestic corporation for the first taxable year for which that percentage is zero.\n\n**(e-2) The conversion is not a taxable event.** **The change in status effected by subsection (e-1) is not a transaction described in section 367 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, gives rise to no deemed distribution, inclusion, gain, or disposition, and does not accelerate any item.** Earnings and profits and adjusted basis carry over unchanged. The Secretary shall prescribe rules for the treatment of previously taxed earnings and profits and of foreign tax credit carryovers on the conversion.\n\n**(f) Anti-substitution.** **The phase-out provided by subsection (b) applies to any provision of law, whatever its form, title or characterization, which has the effect of excluding from Federal gross income, or of exempting from Federal income tax, income derived from sources within the State by reason of the source of that income or the residence of the taxpayer within the State.** A provision described in this subsection which is enacted or invoked after the date of enactment of this Act shall be applied so as to produce the same result as subsection (b), and the Secretary of the Treasury shall publish a statement identifying the provision and the difference between its conditions and those of subsection (b).\n\n**(g) Puerto Rico's own earned income credit payments.** Section 7530 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, under which the Secretary makes matching payments to Puerto Rico for its earned income tax credit, ceases to apply for calendar years beginning after the applicable percentage reaches zero, and applies for each earlier calendar year reduced by the portion of the credit allowed to residents of the State under section 32 by reason of subsection (b)(3)(E)."},{"anchor":"sec-20","heading":"\u00a7 20. Transition to independence","part":"TITLE II \u2014 INDEPENDENCE","body":"**(a) Effective date.** Independence takes effect on the date on which the government of the nation of Puerto Rico takes office under \u00a7 22(c), which shall be not later than four years after certification of the plebiscite results. **If that government has not taken office by the end of that period, sovereignty transfers by operation of this subsection on the last day of that period to the government of Puerto Rico then constituted under its existing constitution, which continues until superseded under a constitution adopted pursuant to this Title.** The repetition provided by subsection (d) does not extend the period fixed by this subsection.\n\n**(b) Constitutional convention.** Not later than 6 months after certification, the legislature of Puerto Rico shall provide for the election of delegates to a constitutional convention. All eligible voters may vote in that election. The delegates shall meet not later than 3 months after their election, and that meeting establishes the convention.\n\n**(c) Character of the constitution.** The convention shall draft a constitution guaranteeing due process and equal protection; freedom of speech, press, assembly, association and religion; the rights of the accused; such other economic, social and cultural rights as the convention deems appropriate; and provisions ensuring that no individual born in Puerto Rico is stateless at birth.\n\n**(d) Ratification.** Not later than one year after the convention is established, the constitution shall be submitted to the eligible voters for ratification or rejection at a special election held as the legislature of Puerto Rico prescribes. If it is rejected, the process is repeated.\n\n**(e) Election of officers.** Not later than one month after ratification, the Governor shall call an election of the officers required by the ratified constitution, to be held not later than 6 months after ratification. The Elections Commission shall certify the results within 10 days.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 INDEPENDENCE","body":"*This Title takes effect only if the status option stated in \u00a7 5(c) is approved.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-21","heading":"\u00a7 21. Joint Transition Commission","part":"TITLE II \u2014 INDEPENDENCE","body":"Not later than 3 months after the convention is established, a Joint Transition Commission shall be appointed in equal numbers by the President and by the presiding officer of the convention. The Commission is responsible for the orderly transfer to the nation of Puerto Rico of the functions exercised by the Federal Government in or with respect to Puerto Rico, and shall recommend to Congress any legislation required. The government of Puerto Rico and the agencies of the United States shall collaborate with the Commission."},{"anchor":"sec-22","heading":"\u00a7 22. Recognition and transfer of sovereignty","part":"TITLE II \u2014 INDEPENDENCE","body":"**(a) Proclamation.** Not later than one month after certification of the election of officers, the President shall by proclamation withdraw and surrender all rights of possession, supervision, jurisdiction, control and sovereignty then exercised by the United States over the territory and residents of Puerto Rico, and recognize the independence of the nation of Puerto Rico.\n\n**(b) Failure to proclaim \u2014 what occurs by operation of law, and what does not.** **If the President has not issued the proclamation within the period stated, the withdrawal and surrender of all rights of possession, supervision, jurisdiction, control and sovereignty of the United States over the territory and residents of Puerto Rico occur by operation of this subsection** on the last day of that period, with the same effect as if proclaimed. **The withdrawal so effected is an exercise of the power of Congress to dispose of the territory of the United States under clause 2 of section 3 of article IV of the Constitution.**\n\n**(b-1) Recognition is not effected by this Act.** **The recognition of the nation of Puerto Rico as a foreign sovereign, and the recognition of its government, are committed to the President by the Constitution, and nothing in this Act effects, directs, compels, or purports to effect a recognition.** **The withdrawal of the sovereignty of the United States under subsection (a) or (b) is complete whether or not the President recognizes the nation of Puerto Rico, and the absence of recognition does not restore, continue, or revive the sovereignty of the United States over Puerto Rico.**\n\n**(c) Date government takes office.** Not later than one week after the proclamation is issued or the period under subsection (b) expires, the presiding officer of the convention, with the advice of the officer elected as head of state, shall fix the date on which the government of the nation takes office, and shall notify the Governor, the President, the President pro tempore of the Senate and the Speaker of the House of Representatives.\n\n**(d) Property and law.** Upon independence, and except as this Title or a later agreement between the two nations provides, all property, rights and interests acquired by the United States over Puerto Rico by virtue of the Treaty of Paris of 1898 and thereafter, other than property lawfully disposed of before independence, vest in the nation of Puerto Rico; and the laws of the United States applicable to the territory cease to apply, except as \u00a7 23 provides.\n\n**(e) Judicial matters.** The nation of Puerto Rico shall recognize and give effect to orders and judgments rendered before independence. Proceedings pending in the courts of the territory continue in the corresponding courts of the nation. Upon independence the judicial power of the United States no longer extends to Puerto Rico, and pending proceedings in the United States District Court for the District of Puerto Rico transfer to the competent judicial authority of the nation, except that where the United States or an officer of the United States is a party, a final judgment is executed by the competent authority of the United States."},{"anchor":"sec-23","heading":"\u00a7 23. Citizenship and migration after independence","part":"TITLE II \u2014 INDEPENDENCE","body":"**(a) Nationality of the nation.** After independence, the citizenship status of an individual born in Puerto Rico is determined by the constitution and laws of the nation of Puerto Rico.\n\n**(b) Retention.** A person holding United States citizenship on the date of independence **retains that citizenship for life.** The conferral of citizenship by the laws of Puerto Rico is not a basis for loss or relinquishment of United States citizenship under section 349 of the Immigration and Nationality Act.\n\n**(c) Birth after independence.** An individual born in Puerto Rico after independence to at least one parent who acquired United States citizenship under section 302 of the Immigration and Nationality Act is not a United States citizen at birth under subsection (c), (d) or (g) of section 301 of that Act.\n\n**(d) Travel and work.** A person who acquires the citizenship of Puerto Rico at birth on or after independence, and a naturalized citizen of Puerto Rico who has been an actual resident there for not less than five years after naturalization and holds proof of that residence, may enter, engage in occupations, and establish residence as a nonimmigrant in the United States and its territories without regard to paragraphs (5)(A) and (7) of section 212(a) of the Immigration and Nationality Act, and is considered to have permission to accept employment. The right to establish habitual residence in a territory or possession may be subject to nondiscriminatory limitations provided by law. This subsection expires 25 years after the date of independence.\n\n**(e) Immigration laws otherwise applicable.** Except as this section provides, after independence citizens of Puerto Rico seeking to enter or to obtain citizenship in the United States are subject to the immigration laws of the United States."},{"anchor":"sec-24","heading":"\u00a7 24. Vested benefits and transitional assistance after independence","part":"TITLE II \u2014 INDEPENDENCE","body":"**(a) Vested rights.** Rights and benefits accruing to residents of the territory under the laws of the United States from past service or contributions \u2014 including benefits for veterans and their relatives, retired Government employees, and beneficiaries of old-age, disability or survivors insurance \u2014 **are not interrupted by independence** and continue until extinguished according to the applicable laws of the United States. Services required as part of those rights are made available through the government of the nation as the two nations agree.\n\n**(b) Social security contributions.** Contributions made by employees and employers in Puerto Rico with respect to persons who upon independence are residents of the nation and are not yet eligible for benefits shall be transferred to the government of the nation once it establishes its own social security system, may be used for no other purpose, and upon transfer the obligations of the United States under the Social Security Act with respect to those persons cease.\n\n**(c) Block grants.** Other Federal transfer payments to individuals and to the government of the territory are maintained as annual block grants used at the discretion of the government of the nation. For the ten fiscal years following independence, the annual grant equals the greater of the aggregate annual funding of all programs then extending to the territory or of all programs extending to it in the fiscal year immediately before independence. Beginning in the eleventh fiscal year the grant decreases on a straight-line basis at ten percent each year. The terms of this subsection may be modified by agreement between the two nations."},{"anchor":"sec-30","heading":"\u00a7 30. Transition to free association","part":"TITLE III \u2014 SOVEREIGNTY IN FREE ASSOCIATION","body":"**(a) Effective date.** Sovereignty in free association takes effect on the date on which the government of the nation of Puerto Rico takes office, which shall be not later than five years after certification of the plebiscite results. **If that government has not taken office by the end of that period, sovereignty transfers by operation of this subsection on the last day of that period to the government of Puerto Rico then constituted under its existing constitution, which continues until superseded.** **If the Articles of Free Association have not entered into force by that date, sovereignty transfers notwithstanding, and \u00a7\u00a7 23 and 24 govern the relationship between the two nations until the Articles enter into force.**\n\n**(b) Constitutional convention; ratification; officers.** Sections 20(b) through 20(e) apply, except that the constitution shall be submitted for ratification not later than two years after the convention is established, and the ratification vote shall include a separate vote on the Articles of Free Association under \u00a7 32(a).\n\n**(c) Recognition.** Section 22 applies, except that the proclamation recognizes the sovereignty of the nation of Puerto Rico in free association with the United States.\n\n**(d) Property, law and judicial matters.** Subsections (d) and (e) of \u00a7 22 apply, except that the laws of the United States continue to apply to the extent the Articles of Free Association provide.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 SOVEREIGNTY IN FREE ASSOCIATION","body":"*This Title takes effect only if the status option stated in \u00a7 5(d) is approved.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-31","heading":"\u00a7 31. Bilateral Negotiating Commission","part":"TITLE III \u2014 SOVEREIGNTY IN FREE ASSOCIATION","body":"**(a) Establishment.** Not later than 3 months after the convention is established, the convention shall elect 5 of its delegates to the Bilateral Negotiating Commission, and the President shall designate 5 members, one of whom shall be nominated for the rank of Ambassador. The members shall meet not later than 3 months after their selection.\n\n**(b) Duties.** The Commission shall negotiate the Articles of Free Association, addressing foreign affairs, trade, finance, taxation, currency, economic assistance, security and defense, migration, economic benefits, dispute resolution and termination; shall provide for the orderly transfer of functions; and shall endeavor to complete the Articles not later than two years after the convention commences."},{"anchor":"sec-32","heading":"\u00a7 32. Articles of Free Association","part":"TITLE III \u2014 SOVEREIGNTY IN FREE ASSOCIATION","body":"**(a) Approval.** The Articles come into effect upon mutual agreement of the two governments after approval by a separate ratification vote of the eligible voters at the special election held under \u00a7 30(b), and by the Government of the United States in accordance with its constitutional processes. If the voters reject the Articles, the process is repeated.\n\n**(b) Termination.** The Articles may be terminated at will by either party at any time.\n\n**(c) Citizenship and migration.** Section 23 applies, except that during the implementation of the first Articles of Free Association an individual born in Puerto Rico to at least one parent who is a United States citizen is a United States citizen at birth under section 301 of the Immigration and Nationality Act if otherwise eligible; and the travel and work authorization under \u00a7 23(d) expires upon termination of the Articles rather than after 25 years.\n\n**(d) Vested benefits and assistance.** Section 24 applies, and its terms may be revised as part of an agreement under the Articles."},{"anchor":"sec-40","heading":"\u00a7 40. Continuation","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 CONTINUATION OF TERRITORIAL STATUS","body":"**(a) Effect.** Where the status option stated in \u00a7 5(f) is approved, or where no status option is approved, Puerto Rico continues as a territory of the United States under the law in force on the day before the plebiscite, and Titles I, II and III do not take effect.\n\n**(b) No determination of permanence.** **The continuation of territorial status under this section is not a determination by Congress that territorial status is permanent, is not a waiver by the residents of Puerto Rico of any claim to self-determination, and does not bar a subsequent Act of Congress providing for a further plebiscite.**\n\n**(c) Report.** Not later than ten years after a plebiscite in which no status option is approved, the Elections Commission may certify to Congress a petition of the eligible voters requesting a further plebiscite, and Congress shall consider it.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 CONTINUATION OF TERRITORIAL STATUS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-50","heading":"\u00a7 50. Effective dates","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"This Act takes effect on the date of its enactment. Titles I, II and III take effect only as \u00a7 4(f) provides.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-51","heading":"\u00a7 51. Severability","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.** If a provision of this Act, or its application to a person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and its application to other persons and circumstances are not affected.\n\n**(b) The transition is not aggregated with the remainder.** **The admission of the State by \u00a7 11, the recognition of independence by \u00a7 22, and the recognition of sovereignty in free association by \u00a7 30(c) are each independent of every other provision of this Act.** A holding that any other provision is invalid, or an order enjoining any other provision, does not affect the transition so effected, the entitlement of the State's Senators and Representatives to their seats, or the equal footing of the State. **It is the intent of Congress that this Act not be treated as an integrated scheme whose provisions stand or fall together, and that each transition named in this subsection be given effect independently of every other provision.**\n\n**(c) No condition upon the State.** No provision of this Act is a condition of the admission of the State, and no provision of this Act restricts the exercise by the State of any power possessed by the other States."},{"anchor":"sec-52","heading":"\u00a7 52. Rules of construction","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Self-determination.** Nothing in this Act diminishes the right of the residents of Puerto Rico to self-determination.\n\n**(b) Citizenship.** No provision of this Act operates to terminate United States citizenship lawfully acquired, except as \u00a7 23(c) and \u00a7 32(c) expressly provide with respect to persons born after a transition date.\n\n**(c) No implied repeal of vested rights.** A benefit vested before a transition date is not extinguished by that transition."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 16A(b) period for an initial determination on an application made on the basis of disability | 90 days from the application | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 16A(b) period for any other initial determination on an application under a program named in \u00a7 16 | 30 days from the application | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 16A(e) share of an agency's determinations in a quarter made outside the period in S-1 or S-2 that constitutes a pattern | 5 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 15(c) provisions of law to which \u00a7 15(a) does not apply, and the reference that governs each | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 15(a-1) provisions distinguishing Puerto Rico from the States that survive the admission date | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-13"}}}};
